# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 1
R.chapter_heading("Introduction", new_page=False)
R.p("Chapter 1 sets out the problem this project addresses: an obligation issued by the Reserve Bank of India often "
    "appears in the Master Directions of several classes of regulated entity, and the copies do not always say the "
    "same thing. The chapter gives the regulatory background, explains why such differences matter to compliance "
    "teams, states the research questions, lists what the project contributes and outlines the remaining chapters.")
R.h2("1.1 Background of the project topic")
R.p("The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) is India's central bank and the main regulator of its banking and non-banking "
    "financial sector [@rbi]. It consolidates its binding instructions into *Master Directions*. Each Direction covers "
    "one subject, such as Know Your Customer norms, outsourcing of financial services, interest rates on deposits or "
    "priority-sector lending, and is addressed to one or more classes of regulated entity. Commercial banks, small "
    "finance banks, payments banks, urban and rural co-operative banks, non-banking financial companies, all-India "
    "financial institutions and asset reconstruction companies each receive their own Directions. Amendments are "
    "usually written into the consolidated Direction itself, which is then stamped “Updated as on” a date.")
R.p("Because the RBI writes a separate Direction for each class, one subject can produce several near-parallel texts. "
    "In most places the wording barely changes. In others the substance does: a monetary threshold is higher, a "
    "reporting deadline is shorter, a different body must approve the policy, or a carve-out applies to one class "
    "only. These are the differences that a financial group running a bank and an NBFC, an auditor reviewing several "
    "classes, or a regulatory-technology vendor has to track.")
R.p("Pre-trained transformers [@vaswani2017, @devlin2019bert, @legalbert] and benchmark suites such as LexGLUE "
    "[@chalkidis2022lexglue] have made legal and regulatory text a mainstream NLP domain. For regulatory question "
    "answering the usual design is retrieval-augmented generation (RAG): a retriever finds evidence passages and a "
    "large language model (LLM) answers from them [@lewis2020rag]. The field also has dedicated datasets and metrics, "
    "most prominently ObliQA and the RePASs answer-stability score [@gokhan2024regnlp, @repass].")
R.h2("1.2 Motivation and scope of the report")
R.p("Errors in compliance work are costly in both directions. A reported difference that does not exist sends "
    "reviewers chasing nothing; a real difference that goes unreported leaves a gap that can lead to supervisory "
    "action and monetary penalties. If a system answers “the same rule applies” when the threshold has in fact "
    "changed, it has hidden exactly the information the compliance officer was looking for. Generic regulatory QA "
    "benchmarks check whether an answer is faithful to the passage it retrieved. They do not check whether that "
    "passage is the right one for the asker's entity class, or whether two parallel passages impose the same "
    "obligation.")
R.p("The project's main output is a benchmark and the evaluation built on it. The class- and date-aware RAG pipeline "
    "is an engineering artefact that we evaluate on the benchmark, not the contribution itself. The scope is all 381 "
    "Master Directions that the RBI listed when we harvested the corpus, the eleven regulated-entity classes in that "
    "listing, and English text only. Circulars that have not been consolidated into a Master Direction are out of "
    "scope, and nothing in the project is legal advice. Two applications were also delivered: a public website for "
    "inspecting the benchmark results, and a local compliance audit generator intended for a regulated entity's "
    "compliance team.")
R.h2("1.3 Problem statement")
R.p("**Given an obligation in the Master Direction of one entity class, the task is to decide, for every other "
    "class, whether a counterpart obligation exists and whether it imposes the same requirement or a different one; "
    "to retrieve that counterpart; to determine whether a paragraph is in force on a given date; and to test whether "
    "the standard faithfulness metric for regulatory answers can be gamed on Indian regulatory text.**")
R.p("Three research questions follow from this problem:")
R.bullets([
    "**RQ1 (Applicability):** Given a compliance question and a declared entity class, can a retrieval system return "
    "the obligations that bind that class, and how much does a class-blind dense retriever lose relative to a "
    "class-aware system?",
    "**RQ2 (Differential obligation):** For a subject issued in parallel across entity classes, can the system "
    "identify what one entity class is required to do that another entity class is not?",
    "**RQ3 (Metric integrity):** Does the documented gameability of RePASs reproduce on Indian regulatory text, and "
    "what minimal evaluation repair makes the metric resistant to concatenation or verbatim extraction?",
])
R.h2("1.4 Salient contribution")
R.bullets([
    "A pointer-only corpus of all 381 RBI Master Directions (76,188 paragraphs with stable identifiers), organised by "
    "an entity class × subject family matrix.",
    "**T1**: 106 expert-validated items and 412 class judgements for applicability and differential obligations, "
    "published with a precise account of their reliability evidence.",
    "**T2**: three relevance sets for cross-class counterpart retrieval, each with its construction bias documented.",
    "**T3**, a temporal-status set derived deterministically from Amendment Directions, and **T4**, an audit of the "
    "RePASs metric on RBI text.",
    "An evaluation of class- and date-aware retrieval and judgement systems using paired bootstrap confidence "
    "intervals, including a pre-registered **structured comparator** aimed at the *different* failure.",
    "Two applications on top of the benchmark: a public inspection website with a live comparator API, and a local, "
    "citation-backed **compliance audit generator** with an on-device AI assistant.",
    "A research paper submitted to AISIGH-SD 2026, an IEEE-sponsored conference.",
])
R.h2("1.5 Organization of report")
R.p("Chapter 2 reviews work on regulatory NLP, retrieval and RAG evaluation and locates the gap this project fills. "
    "Chapter 3 explains how the system was built: the block diagram, hardware and software, corpus construction, "
    "benchmark construction and annotation, the retrieval and judgement systems, the structured comparator, the "
    "evaluation protocol, the two applications and the standards we followed. Chapter 4 reports and analyses the "
    "results question by question. Chapter 5 weighs advantages, limitations and applications, and Chapter 6 "
    "concludes and sets out future work. Appendices A to D hold the algorithm flowcharts, data sheets for the dataset "
    "and models, the software components and the list of papers; the annexures hold the topic approval form, the "
    "weekly log book and the proof of paper submission.")
R.p("**Summary.** The RBI repeats many obligations across the Directions of different entity classes, and the places "
    "where the copies differ are the ones compliance teams care about most. RBI-ObliBench is built to test whether "
    "retrieval and LLM systems can find those differences, and the three research questions frame that test around "
    "applicability, differential obligations and metric integrity.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 2
R.chapter_heading("Literature Survey")
R.p("Four strands of earlier work shape this project: language models for legal text, lexical and dense retrieval, "
    "retrieval-augmented generation and its evaluation, and the statistics of annotation and benchmarking. Section "
    "2.1 sets out this background, Section 2.2 examines the most closely related studies one by one, Section 2.3 "
    "compares them in a table, and Section 2.4 states the gap that RBI-ObliBench addresses.")
R.h2("2.1 Introduction to the overall topic")
R.p("**Language models for legal and regulatory text.** The transformer [@vaswani2017] and BERT's bidirectional "
    "pre-training [@devlin2019bert] reset the baseline for most NLP tasks, and domain adaptation soon followed. "
    "LEGAL-BERT, pre-trained on legislation, court cases and contracts, improved legal classification over general "
    "BERT [@legalbert], and LexGLUE gathered seven English legal-understanding tasks into one benchmark, on which "
    "legal-domain models led but still left substantial headroom [@chalkidis2022lexglue]. Regulatory text has "
    "difficulties of its own: deontic language (“shall”, “must”, “is required to”), long nested "
    "clauses, defined terms and cross-references. The deontic cues matter directly here, because our candidate "
    "extractor relies on them (Section 3.4).")
R.p("**Lexical and dense retrieval.** BM25, derived from the probabilistic relevance framework, remains a strong "
    "passage-retrieval baseline [@robertson2009bm25], and we use the BM25S implementation for speed [@bm25s]. Dense "
    "retrieval instead encodes queries and passages with neural encoders. Dense Passage Retrieval showed that a dual "
    "encoder can beat BM25 on open-domain QA [@karpukhin2020dpr], Sentence-BERT made sentence embeddings cheap to "
    "compute [@sbert], and the BGE family from C-Pack offers strong general-purpose English embeddings [@bge]. "
    "Reciprocal rank fusion merges ranked lists without calibrating their scores and often outperforms each input "
    "[@rrf], while a BERT cross-encoder can re-score a short candidate list jointly with the query "
    "[@nogueira2019passage]. Graded measures such as nDCG are the standard way to evaluate the resulting rankings "
    "[@jarvelin2002ndcg].")
R.p("**Retrieval-augmented generation and its evaluation.** RAG conditions a generator on retrieved passages "
    "[@lewis2020rag], so its evaluation has to separate the quality of retrieval from the quality of the answer. "
    "Reference-free frameworks such as RAGAS use LLM judges to score faithfulness and answer relevance [@es2024ragas]. "
    "Faithfulness checks frequently rest on natural language inference (NLI) models trained on SNLI and MultiNLI "
    "[@bowman2015snli, @williams2018mnli], including DeBERTa-v3 cross-encoders [@debertav3, @nlideberta]. Long-context "
    "models are an alternative to retrieval, but they use information in the middle of a long input less reliably "
    "than information at its start or end [@liu2024lost]. Open and hosted models such as gpt-oss-120b [@gptoss] and "
    "Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite [@gemini35fl] have made such systems inexpensive to run.")
R.p("**Annotation and statistical evaluation.** A benchmark's labels are only as useful as the evidence for their "
    "reliability. Cohen's κ measures chance-corrected agreement between two raters [@cohen1960], Fleiss' κ "
    "generalises it to several raters [@fleiss1971], and Landis and Koch's scale is the usual guide to interpreting "
    "the values [@landis1977]. Wilson's score interval gives dependable confidence intervals for proportions "
    "[@wilson1927], and the bootstrap provides intervals for statistics such as F1 and nDCG that have no simple "
    "closed form [@efron1979]. Datasheets for datasets recommend recording a dataset's motivation, composition, "
    "collection process and intended use [@gebru2021datasheets]; Appendix B follows that practice.")
R.h2("2.2 Survey of closely related work")
R.p("**Gokhan et al. (2024), RIRAG and ObliQA** [@gokhan2024regnlp]. This work defined the two-stage regulatory RAG "
    "task that later studies, including ours, build on: retrieve the relevant passages, then generate an answer. It "
    "introduced ObliQA, 27,869 questions over the financial regulations of the Abu Dhabi Global Market, and RePASs, a "
    "reference-free score that combines entailment, contradiction and obligation coverage computed with NLI and an "
    "obligation classifier. ObliQA comes from a single regulator and jurisdiction, and each question is tied to one "
    "regulatory context. That design suits question answering over one rulebook, which is the setting our T1 and T2 "
    "tasks move away from.")
R.p("**RegNLP, RePASs implementation** [@repass]. The released code computes RePASs with an NLI cross-encoder and an "
    "obligation classifier fine-tuned from LEGAL-BERT [@legalbert]. We ran it unmodified at a pinned commit, which is "
    "what makes our metric audit (T4) reproducible.")
R.p("**Gokhan et al. (2025), RIRAG-2025 shared task** [@gokhan2025riragtask]. The shared task at the first Regulatory "
    "NLP workshop evaluated retrieval and generation systems from several teams on ObliQA, with RePASs as the "
    "official answer metric. For this project its main consequence is that RePASs became the reference metric for "
    "regulatory RAG, which is why its robustness is worth testing.")
R.p("**Chasandras et al. (2025), AUEB-Archimedes at RIRAG-2025** [@aueb2025rirag]. This team found that concatenating "
    "the obligation sentences of the retrieved passages, with no generation step, already scores highly under "
    "RePASs. The finding exposes a structural weakness of a metric built on entailment and coverage: copying the "
    "evidence is rewarded. It was shown on ObliQA only, so our RQ3 asks whether it reproduces on RBI text.")
R.p("**Gokhan and Briscoe (2025), ObliQA-MP** [@obliqamp]. ObliQA-MP extends ObliQA to answers grounded in several "
    "passages and applies learning-to-rank to regulatory RAG. Multi-passage answers are closer to real compliance "
    "questions, which often draw on more than one provision; the passages, however, still come from a single "
    "rulebook.")
R.p("**Pall (2026), IndiaFinBench** [@indiafinbench]. IndiaFinBench evaluates LLMs on 406 expert-annotated questions "
    "over SEBI and RBI text, covering regulatory interpretation, numerical reasoning, contradiction and temporal "
    "questions. It is the Indian benchmark closest to ours. Its questions test the understanding of individual "
    "documents, so it complements a cross-document task such as T1, and we use it as an external check in Section 4.9.")
R.p("**Xiao et al. (2024), C-Pack and BGE embeddings** [@bge]. C-Pack released the BGE models and their training "
    "recipe; we use bge-base-en-v1.5 both for cross-class matching and as the dense retriever. General-purpose "
    "embeddings are good at finding paragraphs on the same topic. T1 asks a harder question of them: whether they can "
    "separate “same topic, same rule” from “same topic, different rule”.")
R.p("**Cormack et al. (2009), reciprocal rank fusion** [@rrf]. RRF scores each document by summing 1/(k + rank) over "
    "the input rankings. It needs no training and is robust, which is why our hybrid retriever uses it as the fusion "
    "step (k = 60).")
R.p("**Karpukhin et al. (2020), Dense Passage Retrieval** [@karpukhin2020dpr] and **Lewis et al. (2020), RAG** "
    "[@lewis2020rag]. DPR established dual-encoder retrieval as a serious alternative to BM25, and RAG established the "
    "retrieve-then-generate design. Both treat relevance as a property of the query and the passage alone. In "
    "regulation, relevance also depends on *who* is asking: the correct paragraph for a small finance bank may be the "
    "wrong one for an NBFC. Our class-aware retriever supplies this missing condition through metadata filtering.")
R.p("**Es et al. (2024), RAGAS** [@es2024ragas]. RAGAS defines reference-free metrics for faithfulness, answer "
    "relevance and context relevance. Keeping faithfulness and relevance apart turns out to matter for our T4 result, "
    "in which RePASs tracks human faithfulness ratings but not whether an answer addresses the question.")
R.p("**Liu et al. (2024), Lost in the Middle** [@liu2024lost]. Long-context models use information at the beginning "
    "and end of their input better than information in the middle. This motivated our long-context baseline, which "
    "reads every Direction of the compared classes up to a 100K-token cap, as a direct test of whether retrieval is "
    "needed at all.")
R.p("**Chalkidis et al. (2020, 2022), LEGAL-BERT and LexGLUE** [@legalbert, @chalkidis2022lexglue]. These studies "
    "show that legal language benefits from domain-specific models and that legal-understanding benchmarks remain "
    "difficult. Their tasks centre on court decisions and contracts; the obligations that a regulator places on "
    "supervised entities are not their focus.")
R.h2("2.3 Comparison of the surveyed work")
R.p("Table 2.1 places the surveyed work along the two dimensions that matter most for this project: whether it "
    "compares obligations across entity classes, and whether it examines the answer metric itself.")
@@B1@@
R.h2("2.4 Research gap and problem definition")
R.p("Taken together, the surveyed work leaves three questions open. First, regulatory QA datasets treat a regulation "
    "as one rulebook, so none of them tests whether a system can recognise that an obligation in one entity class "
    "has a counterpart in another class that says something different. Second, regulatory retrieval is evaluated "
    "without the asker's entity class, even though the correct paragraph depends on it. Third, the concatenation "
    "weakness of RePASs has been shown only on ObliQA, and whether it carries over to Indian regulatory text is "
    "unknown. These three gaps correspond, in order, to RQ2, RQ1 and RQ3 in Section 1.3.")
R.p("**Summary.** Retrieval and RAG methods for regulatory text are well developed, and ObliQA with RePASs is the "
    "reference evaluation. What the literature lacks is a test of cross-class differences, class-conditioned "
    "retrieval and an independent check of RePASs; RBI-ObliBench is designed around those three needs.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 3
R.chapter_heading("Methodology and Implementation")
R.p("This chapter describes what we built and how each part works, in the order the pipeline runs: harvesting the "
    "RBI corpus, extracting obligations, matching them across classes, annotating them, and then running the "
    "retrieval and judgement systems, the structured comparator and the evaluation protocol. It closes with the two "
    "applications, the standards we followed, our use of AI tools and the project schedule.")
R.h2("3.1 Block diagram")
R.p("Fig. 3.1 shows the complete system in three stages. The top row builds the corpus: Master Directions are "
    "downloaded from the RBI website, converted to text, split into paragraphs with stable identifiers and tagged with "
    "entity class and subject family. The middle row builds the benchmark: obligation candidates are extracted and "
    "matched across classes, an expert labels them, and supplementary sources feed the temporal and retrieval sets. "
    "The bottom row runs the systems on the benchmark and produces the paper, the data release and the applications.")
@@B2@@
R.p("Fig. 3.2, taken from the research paper, shows the benchmark-construction steps in more detail.")
@@B3@@
R.h2("3.2 Hardware and software description")
R.p("The project is software and data only and needed no special hardware. All development and CPU work ran on an "
    "Apple-silicon (arm64) laptop under macOS. The cross-class embedding step ran on Kaggle Notebooks; when the code "
    "runs there, its path resolver reads from the Kaggle inputs and writes only to the Kaggle working directory. LLM "
    "calls went to hosted APIs on free tiers (Google Gemini API and Groq). The public demonstration website is an "
    "Azure Storage static website, and its comparator API is an Azure Function. The audit generator, by contrast, "
    "runs entirely on the user's own machine, and its AI assistant uses a local Ollama model. Tables 3.1 and 3.2 list "
    "the platforms and the main software versions.")
@@B4@@
@@B5@@
R.h2("3.3 Corpus construction")
R.p("A scraper read the RBI's Master Directions listing and downloaded all 381 Directions. We extracted the text from "
    "each PDF and split it into 76,188 paragraphs, each with a stable identifier of the form md_NNNNN::pNNNNN and its "
    "clause path. An earlier harvest had missed 81 Directions. The repair phase (P1-004) recovered them using "
    "retries, a manual import path and consistency gates, so every later artefact is built on the full set of 381.")
R.p("The RBI listing groups Directions under 19 headings: 11 regulated-entity classes (among them commercial banks, "
    "small finance banks, payments banks, urban and rural co-operative banks, NBFCs and asset reconstruction "
    "companies) and 8 RBI function headings. Deriving subject families from the titles gave 57 families, and the "
    "resulting entity class × subject family matrix has 1,083 cells, of which 296 are populated and 9 ambiguous. "
    "Because the RBI consolidates amendments in place, 162 titles carry an “Updated as on” stamp and the "
    "corpus contains only current text. For validation we also harvested supplementary sources: 80 FAQ pages with "
    "1,845 items, 463 enforcement press releases (with the entity class resolved for 455) and 333 pages of Amendment "
    "Directions.")
R.h2("3.4 Obligation candidate extraction")
R.p("The extractor is rule-based. It marks a paragraph as an obligation candidate when the paragraph contains a "
    "deontic cue such as “shall”, “must”, “is required to” or “should ensure”, and it "
    "excludes definitions, duties placed on customers and purely descriptive text. Of the 33,507 paragraphs that "
    "contain a deontic cue, the final rules (v4) keep 28,694 candidates. We refined the rules over four precision "
    "rounds, checking each version by hand on a fresh sample (Section 4.2).")
R.h2("3.5 Cross-class matching")
R.p("Each candidate obligation was matched to counterpart paragraphs in the Directions that the other entity classes "
    "receive on the same subject. The matcher combines bge-base-en-v1.5 cosine similarity with lexical overlap, and "
    "its threshold of 0.757567 was fixed on validation pairs before the matcher was used. The top two matches for each "
    "compared class later became the candidates shown to the annotator. Used directly as labels, the same matches form "
    "the “P2-002 candidates” baseline.")
R.h2("3.6 Benchmark tasks and annotation protocol")
R.p("**T1 (RQ1, RQ2).** We drew 123 candidates with a fixed seed, stratified by source class, with at most four "
    "compared classes per item. For each compared class the annotator chooses one of three judgements and cites the "
    "counterpart paragraph: **same** (the same obligation in substance), **different** (the same topic but a different "
    "threshold, timeline, scope, actor or carve-out) or **none** (no counterpart among the paragraphs checked). A span "
    "that imposes no duty on a regulated entity is rejected as not an obligation. Guideline rule v3.6 then derives the "
    "item-level differential flag: any *different* gives *class-specific*; otherwise any *same* gives *shared*; and "
    "all *none* gives *absent*. Of the 123 items, 106 were validated and 17 rejected, leaving 412 class judgements.")
R.p("**Annotation workflow.** The gold labels come from one expert annotator who worked on blind sheets with written "
    "guidelines. Of the 106 gold items, 62 are single-pass, 6 were consistent across a blind retest, and 38 were "
    "adjudicated by the same annotator. Reliability was measured separately from the gold: three raters, all of them "
    "student authors, independently labelled thirty items drawn outside the T1 sample, and the annotator repeated a "
    "blind test–retest on 25 items. Fig. 3.3 shows this provenance.")
@@B6@@
R.p("**T2 (RQ1)** has three relevance sets for retrieving the target-class counterpart: one derived from the T1 "
    "labels (291 queries), one from the P2-002 validation pairs (53) and one from a hand alignment of FAQ and press "
    "release questions (25). **T3** asks for the status of a paragraph (in force, amended or deleted) on an as-of "
    "date; its 52 scored items come from a deterministic parser of Amendment Directions and update stamps. **T4 "
    "(RQ3)** has 30 questions. Each has a genuine RAG answer and three rule-built variants: a concatenation of the "
    "retrieved obligation sentences, a negated version of that concatenation, and an off-topic answer of matched "
    "length. Thirty answers also carry blind human ratings of faithfulness and of whether they answer the question. "
    "Table 3.3 summarises the benchmark.")
@@B7@@
R.h2("3.7 Retrieval and judgement systems")
R.p("Fig. 3.4 shows the evaluation architecture. We compared five retrievers: BM25; dense retrieval with "
    "bge-base-en-v1.5; a hybrid that fuses the two with RRF (k = 60); a **class-aware hybrid** restricted to the "
    "Directions of the target class (and its function-heading Directions); and, as a diagnostic, the class-blind "
    "hybrid with every paragraph of the query's own source class removed. A **temporal filter** keeps only paragraphs "
    "in force on the as-of date, using the same amendment index that builds T3.")
@@B8@@
R.p("All T1 judgement systems use gemini-3.5-flash-lite at temperature zero, with minimal thinking, JSON output and "
    "frozen prompts whose SHA-256 hashes are stored in the configuration. They differ in the evidence the model sees "
    "and in whether its answer is checked:")
R.bullets([
    "a **class-blind LLM** and a **class-aware LLM**, each judging the retrieved evidence;",
    "the **final agentic system**, which adds to the class filter an NLI verifier (nli-deberta-v3-xsmall) that retries "
    "or abstains when the cited paragraph does not entail the rationale;",
    "a **class-blind LLM with the verifier**;",
    "a **long-context system** that receives every Direction of the compared classes, up to a 100K-token cap.",
])
R.p("Two baselines anchor the comparison: the majority class, computed in-sample from the gold and therefore a "
    "reference point rather than a deployable system, and the P2-002 candidate labels. Ablations A, B and C switch the "
    "class filter, the temporal filter and the verifier on and off. The orchestration is plain Python; a later "
    "LangGraph port reproduced all four retrieval-based T1 systems exactly. The final agentic system runs as follows:")
R.bullets([
    "Input: source obligation *o*, compared class *c*, as-of date *t*.",
    "Retrieve the top-k paragraphs for *o* with the hybrid retriever, restricted to class *c* (class filter) and to "
    "paragraphs in force on *t* (temporal filter).",
    "Ask the LLM for a judgement in {same, different, none}, the matched paragraph id and a one-sentence rationale.",
    "Check the rationale against the cited paragraph with the NLI verifier; if it is not entailed, retry once with "
    "the verifier's feedback; if it still fails, abstain.",
    "Derive the item flag from the per-class judgements with rule v3.6.",
], numbered=True)
R.h2("3.8 Pairwise structured comparator")
R.p("The comparator is this project's targeted experiment, designed for the *different* failure. For each item and "
    "compared class, the model receives the source obligation and the two candidate paragraphs that the annotator was "
    "shown. It extracts thirteen fields from both sides (actor, action, object, scope, condition, threshold, amount, "
    "percentage, frequency, deadline, exception, documentation and reporting) and marks each field as equal, "
    "different or present on one side only. The verdict is then decided by a **fixed rule, not by the model**, with "
    "the precedence same > different > none. We developed the prompt and the rule only on the separate 30-item set, "
    "revised them once to fix a field-extraction bug, and froze them by hash (system prompt 51f7ea78ed4b…, rule "
    "83664f8d9d8f…) before any run on the T1 gold. The comparisons against the class-aware LLM and against "
    "P2-002 were pre-registered.")
R.h2("3.9 Evaluation protocol")
R.p("T1 is scored with five measures: three-way flag macro-F1 (shared, class-specific, absent); collapsed flag "
    "macro-F1 (absent versus present), which is the fair comparison with P2-002 because P2-002 cannot express "
    "*different*; judgement macro-F1 over class units; *different* F1; and coverage. Abstentions are kept in the "
    "denominator. An abstained class judgement is scored as *unsure*, which matches no label, and an item whose flag "
    "cannot be derived counts as wrong. The long-context system is scored on the 102 items it answered, and every "
    "comparison with it uses those same items. T2 is scored with nDCG@10 [@jarvelin2002ndcg], T3 with status exact "
    "match, and T4 with the RePASs composite and its Spearman correlation with human ratings.")
R.p("Every confidence interval is a 95% percentile bootstrap interval over 2,000 item resamples [@efron1979]. "
    "Differences between systems are paired on the same items, and we state whether each interval excludes zero. "
    "Proportions use Wilson intervals [@wilson1927], and agreement uses Cohen's and Fleiss' κ "
    "[@cohen1960, @fleiss1971].")
R.h2("3.10 Applications built on the benchmark")
R.h3("3.10.1 Interactive inspection website and live comparator API")
R.p("A React and TypeScript website presents the 412 expert-labelled comparisons together with every sealed system "
    "answer. Its *Microscope* page shows a single case in full: both paragraph excerpts with RBI links, each system's "
    "answer, what each LLM was given, the 13-field table and the trace of the frozen rule's decision. A *Failure Map* "
    "plots the 78 real differences against all systems. The site also has a spot-the-difference game, an atlas of 11 "
    "institution types × 57 topics, a *Lab* that re-runs a benchmark pair live and compares it with the sealed "
    "answer, a triage view, results and method pages, and a presenter mode. It is deployed as an Azure static "
    "website, and the comparator API, which loads the frozen prompt and rule and checks their hashes, runs as an "
    "Azure Function. Because RBI text is copyrighted, the site shows at most twelve words per excerpt and always links "
    "to the RBI's PDF.")
R.h3("3.10.2 Local compliance audit generator with an AI assistant")
R.p("The audit generator turns the corpus and pipeline into a tool for a regulated entity's compliance team "
    "(Fig. 3.5). Given an entity profile (class, as-of date, last review date and activities), it produces a "
    "citation-backed audit pack with six parts: an applicability register; a change-impact log covering the period "
    "since the last review; a compliance calendar of deadlines and frequencies; a cross-class differential for group "
    "entities; an enforcement-risk view built from penalty press releases; and a policy gap analysis that marks each "
    "applicable obligation as covered, partial or missing in an uploaded policy. A reviewer confirms or disputes each "
    "row, and the pack can be exported to Excel, DOCX, PDF, iCalendar and a JSON audit trail. The FastAPI backend "
    "binds only to 127.0.0.1.")
@@B9@@
R.p("The AI assistant answers questions about a pack, explains obligations, drafts policy clauses, explains changes "
    "and writes an executive memo. It uses a local Ollama model only and refuses non-loopback hosts. Each sentence it "
    "produces must cite RBI text and pass a check by the NLI cross-encoder; any number it states must appear in the "
    "source, and sentences that fail verification are held back. Its suggestions never change a row's review status.")
R.h2("3.11 Standards followed")
R.p("We followed these standards and recommended practices in design, documentation and testing:")
R.bullets([
    "**ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017** (software life-cycle processes): work proceeded in gated phases, P0 to P5, each with "
    "a written plan, an audit and an acceptance checklist.",
    "**ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148:2018** (requirements engineering): the research questions, tasks and acceptance criteria "
    "were written down before implementation, and the comparator analysis was pre-registered.",
    "**IEEE 1016-2009** (software design descriptions): the repository documents module boundaries, data schemas and "
    "decision records (D-01 to D-28 for the demo).",
    "**ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119** (software testing): the code base has over 1,200 automated Python tests and 115 UI tests; "
    "a release check fails on excluded paths, key-like strings, oversized files or long copied runs of RBI text; and "
    "data assertions stop the UI build on any mismatch.",
    "**IEEE 7000-2021** (ethical concerns during system design): the RBI's copyright is respected through a "
    "pointer-only release, prompts contain no personal data, AI use is disclosed, and every audit output states "
    "“decision support only, not legal advice”.",
    "**IEEE reference style** for citations, **ISO 8601** for dates and **RFC 5545** (iCalendar) for the "
    "compliance-calendar export.",
])
R.h2("3.12 Use of AI tools")
R.p("Claude Code (Anthropic) wrote the repository code under the authors' direction: the scraper, preprocessing, "
    "candidate extraction, matching, benchmark tooling, evaluation and statistics scripts, the inspection interface "
    "and the audit generator. It also drafted the internal reports, and Claude was used to draft and edit the text of "
    "this report. The authors reviewed every output. gemini-3.5-flash-lite and gpt-oss-120b are systems under study, "
    "and their outputs are never used as labels; every human label was assigned manually. Prompts contained only "
    "public RBI text and no personal data.")
R.h2("3.13 Project schedule")
R.p("Fig. 3.6 shows the schedule, which we reviewed with the mentor each week and recorded in the log book. The work "
    "was organised in gated phases (P0 to P5); each phase ended with a written report and an acceptance check before "
    "the next began.")
@@B10@@
R.p("**Summary.** The pipeline harvests and structures all 381 Master Directions, extracts obligations and matches "
    "them across classes, and turns the result into four benchmark tasks whose annotation and reliability evidence "
    "are documented separately. Retrieval, LLM, verifier, long-context and structured-comparator systems are evaluated "
    "under a pre-registered, bootstrap-based protocol, and the same pipeline powers both a public inspection website "
    "and a local audit generator.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 4
R.chapter_heading("Results and Analysis")
R.p("This chapter reports every experiment and reads the results against the three research questions. We first "
    "check the intermediate steps (extraction, matching and annotation), then turn to retrieval (RQ1), differential "
    "judgement (RQ2) and the structured comparator, metric integrity (RQ3) and the temporal task, before covering the "
    "additional experiments and discussing what the results do and do not show. Square brackets give 95% confidence "
    "intervals; for each difference we state whether its interval excludes zero.")
R.h2("4.1 Experimental setup")
R.p("Every T1 system used gemini-3.5-flash-lite at temperature zero with frozen prompts. Each system was run once per "
    "item, and its predictions were sealed before scoring. The evaluation harness regenerates each table from the "
    "stored predictions with a single command, and the paper's tables are generated from the same results file "
    "rather than typed in by hand.")
R.h2("4.2 Intermediate-step validation")
R.p("**Candidate extraction.** Over four rounds of hand checks, the precision of the extraction rules rose from 0.675 "
    "to 0.929 (Table 4.1, Fig. 4.1).")
@@B11@@
@@B12@@
R.p("**Cross-class matching.** On 60 validation pairs the matcher found a true counterpart in 53, a rate of 0.883 "
    "[0.778, 0.942]. **Supplementary sources.** FAQ alignment was clean in only 5 of 30 samples (0.167), and "
    "enforcement alignment in 16 of 30 (0.533). Under the locked decision rule, FAQ text is therefore used only for "
    "validation and motivation, and enforcement releases only as Direction-level weak labels. **T1 sampling.** 17 of "
    "the 123 sampled candidates were rejected as not an obligation (0.138 [0.088, 0.210]). The 106 validated items "
    "are flagged shared (52), class-specific (40) and absent (14).")
R.h2("4.3 Annotation reliability")
@@B13@@
R.p("Agreement among the three raters on class judgements is substantial on the Landis and Koch scale "
    "[@landis1977]. On the derived flag it is only moderate, which is expected: a flag compounds the errors of up to "
    "four class judgements. The retest is supplementary evidence, because a gap of 11.7 to 18.6 hours is short enough "
    "for recall to inflate agreement. The gold itself remains single-expert, and we note this wherever T1 results are "
    "reported.")
R.h2("4.4 RQ1: applicability and counterpart retrieval (T2)")
@@B14@@
@@B15@@
R.p("The class filter raises nDCG@10 on t2_xclass_t1 by **+0.451 [+0.421, +0.481]** (excludes 0), and the gain "
    "appears on all three relevance sets: +0.423 [+0.336, +0.514] on t2_xclass_p2002 and +0.171 [+0.072, +0.273] on "
    "t2_supp_p2003, both excluding zero. Some of this gain is built in, because relevance was labelled only for the "
    "target-class counterpart. The third set, however, was constructed independently and still shows a clear gain. "
    "Removing only the source class is not enough (0.464), which suggests that the benefit comes from knowing the "
    "target class rather than merely from avoiding near-duplicates in the source Direction. The class-blind dense "
    "retriever reaches 0.421 against 0.894 for the class-aware hybrid; for the second half of RQ1, then, a class-blind "
    "retriever loses more than half of the achievable nDCG on this set.")
R.p("The picture changes on T1, where the class filter has no significant effect: class-aware minus class-blind "
    "three-way flag F1 is -0.050 [-0.133, +0.027] without the verifier and -0.049 [-0.133, +0.036] with it, and "
    "neither interval excludes zero. Finding the counterpart is not where T1 systems struggle.")
R.h2("4.5 RQ2: differential obligation (T1)")
@@B16@@
R.p("Table 4.4 gives point estimates; Appendix B lists the confidence interval for every cell. No LLM system "
    "significantly beats the P2-002 candidate labels on collapsed flag F1: class-blind minus P2-002 is -0.019 "
    "[-0.114, +0.072] and class-aware minus P2-002 is +0.024 [-0.096, +0.135], and neither interval excludes zero. "
    "On three-way F1 the class-blind LLM does beat P2-002, by +0.143 [+0.054, +0.232], because P2-002 cannot express "
    "*different* at all.")
@@B17@@
R.h3("4.5.1 The different failure")
R.p("*Different* is where the LLM systems fail most. Of the 78 gold *different* class units, **56 are missed by every "
    "LLM system** when all five answered, and 58 when only the systems that answered are counted. We classify an "
    "error as a retrieval failure if the gold matched paragraph was absent from the system's context, and as a "
    "reasoning failure if it was present. Across the five LLM systems, 262 of the 350 *different* errors occurred "
    "with the gold counterpart in context (Table 4.5, Fig. 4.4), so most of these errors are reasoning failures rather "
    "than retrieval failures.")
@@B18@@
@@B19@@
R.p("The systems also err in different directions. On gold *different* units, the class-aware LLM answers *same* 32 "
    "times and *none* 39 times, while the long-context model answers *same* 43 times. Either way, the difference that a "
    "compliance reviewer needs to see is lost.")
R.h3("4.5.2 Verifier and long-context")
R.p("Adding the NLI verifier lowers three-way flag F1 by **-0.068 [-0.132, -0.012]** and coverage by -0.217 [-0.292, "
    "-0.142], both excluding zero; on the class-blind side the loss is -0.069 [-0.129, -0.014]. The final agentic "
    "system abstained on 81 class judgements, 59 of which had been correct before the retry. Our reading is that a "
    "whole-paragraph entailment premise is a poor test for a one-sentence rationale. The long-context model answered "
    "102 items at the 100K cap. On those items it ties the retrieval systems on three-way F1 and beats the final "
    "agentic system on collapsed F1 by +0.126 [+0.016, +0.240]. When the relevant Directions fit in the context "
    "window, retrieval does not appear to be the bottleneck.")
R.h2("4.6 Targeted experiment: the structured comparator")
@@B20@@
R.p("Against the class-aware LLM, the comparator raises *different* F1 by +0.255, *different* recall by +0.397 and "
    "judgement macro-F1 by +0.071, and all three intervals exclude zero (Table 4.6). The gain does not extend to "
    "collapsed flag F1 (+0.030 [-0.061, +0.128]). The comparator recovers 26 of the 56 units that every LLM system "
    "missed, and on the gold its *different* recall is 0.487 at a precision of 0.352. Its *different* F1 on the "
    "development set had been 0.348, so the gold result does not come from tuning on test data. The remaining errors "
    "run *different* → *none* 32 times and *different* → *same* only 6 times: in most failures the model "
    "decides that the two paragraphs do not describe the same duty. This points to topic matching, rather than field "
    "extraction, as the next bottleneck.")
R.h2("4.7 RQ3: metric integrity (T4)")
@@B21@@
@@B22@@
R.p("Concatenation gameability reproduces on RBI text: concatenated answers score +0.375 [+0.325, +0.427] above "
    "genuine answers under RePASs (pre-registered verdict: reproduces). The metric is not fooled by the other two "
    "attacks, since negated and off-topic answers score below genuine ones. RePASs tracks human faithfulness ratings "
    "(ρ = 0.731) but not whether an answer addresses the question (ρ = 0.008). We also tried an exploratory "
    "repair that adds an overlap penalty and a relevance term. It ranks genuine answers first (+0.208 over "
    "concatenated), but it no longer tracks human faithfulness (ρ = -0.126 [-0.477, 0.216]), so it is not usable "
    "in its current form.")
R.h2("4.8 Temporal status (T3) and ablations")
R.p("With the temporal filter on, status exact match is 1.000, but this result is circular: the gold and the filter "
    "share one parser. Without the filter the score falls to 0.538 [0.404, 0.673], and the model answered “in "
    "force, original” on 48 of the 52 items. What T3 shows, then, is that a model uses the dates it is given but "
    "does not infer amendment status on its own. Table 4.8 summarises ablations A to C.")
@@B23@@
R.h2("4.9 Additional experiments")
R.p("The additional experiments were pre-registered after the main runs and used the same prompts and intervals. A "
    "cross-encoder re-ranker (ms-marco-MiniLM-L6-v2 over the hybrid top 20) gave no reliable T2 gain and lowered T1 "
    "class-blind three-way F1 by -0.064 [-0.115, -0.017]. The larger open-weight gpt-oss-120b (class-blind, on the 103 "
    "items within its free-tier limit) tied gemini-3.5-flash-lite on three-way F1 (-0.010 [-0.094, +0.084]). Two "
    "external checks place the harness against published work: on the ObliQA test set our BM25 retrieval reaches "
    "Recall@10 of 75.3 [73.9, 76.8] against a published 76.1, and on a stratified prefix of 268 of the 406 "
    "IndiaFinBench items the frozen answer prompt scores 75.7% [70.3, 80.5].")
R.h2("4.10 Discussion")
R.p("Six findings stand out. We present them as findings about what the benchmark exposes, not as evidence that our "
    "agent wins:")
R.bullets([
    "Class-aware filtering substantially improves counterpart retrieval.",
    "That improvement does not carry over to T1 judgement: locating the counterpart is easier than deciding what "
    "differs.",
    "*Different* is the core failure, and most *different* errors are reasoning failures.",
    "The NLI verifier hurts T1, because it declines many correct answers.",
    "A long-context model without retrieval is competitive when the relevant Directions fit in its window.",
    "RePASs can be gamed by concatenation on RBI text, so a faithfulness score needs a relevance component that "
    "verbatim text cannot satisfy.",
], numbered=True)
R.p("The comparator result adds a constructive point. Making the comparison explicit, field by field, and leaving the "
    "verdict to a fixed rule recovers part of the *different* signal that free-form judgement misses. It does not "
    "close the gap: the comparator still misses 40 of the 78 *different* units.")
R.h2("4.11 Validity of the results")
R.p("Several measures protect the validity of these results. Every prompt, rule and threshold was frozen and hashed "
    "before the test runs, and the comparator comparisons were pre-registered. Differences are paired bootstrap "
    "estimates with 95% intervals, abstentions are scored as errors instead of being dropped, and the paper's numbers "
    "are generated from the results file. The software was tested following the practices of ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119 "
    "(Section 3.11). These measures guard against tuning on test data and against transcription errors; they do not "
    "remove the limitations of a single-expert gold set, which Chapter 5 discusses.")
R.p("**Summary.** For RQ1, class-aware filtering helps retrieval but not judgement. For RQ2, every LLM system misses "
    "most real differences even with the evidence in context, and only the structured comparator recovers a "
    "substantial share. For RQ3, the concatenation weakness of RePASs reproduces on RBI text, and the simple repair we "
    "tried fixes the ranking only at the cost of agreement with human judgement.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 5
R.chapter_heading("Advantages, Limitations and Applications")
R.p("The strengths and weaknesses of the project follow from the results in Chapter 4. This chapter ties each "
    "advantage to a documented feature or result, states the limitations that constrain how far the results "
    "generalise, and describes where the work can be applied.")
R.h2("5.1 Advantages")
R.bullets([
    "**A new evaluation target.** T1 isolates the case that generic regulatory QA does not test: a counterpart exists "
    "in another class but says something different (Section 4.5).",
    "**A complete, current corpus.** All 381 Master Directions with stable paragraph identifiers, an entity class "
    "× subject family matrix and an amendment index (Section 3.3).",
    "**Careful evaluation.** Prompts and rules were frozen, comparisons pre-registered, intervals computed by paired "
    "bootstrap, abstentions scored as errors, and reliability evidence reported with its limits (Sections 3.9 and "
    "4.11).",
    "**An interpretable comparator.** The 13-field table and the fixed rule show *why* two obligations differ, so a "
    "reviewer can check the verdict field by field (Section 3.8).",
    "**A release that respects copyright.** The data is released as pointers with SHA-256 hashes and a rehydration "
    "script; no RBI text is redistributed.",
    "**Reproducible, tested code.** One command regenerates each table, and the code base has over 1,200 automated "
    "tests and a release safety check (Section 3.11).",
    "**Applications that keep data local.** The audit generator and its AI assistant run on the user's machine, and "
    "uploaded policies appear in the audit trail only as hashes (Section 3.10.2).",
])
R.h2("5.2 Limitations")
R.p("The following limitations bound how far the results generalise:")
R.bullets([
    "T1 has 106 items, with 7 to 15 per source class, so per-class results carry wide uncertainty.",
    "The gold labels come from a single expert. Inter-rater agreement was measured on a separate sample, and the "
    "retest gap was short.",
    "Each item compares at most four classes. The annotator and the comparator saw the same two candidate paragraphs, "
    "which may bias both towards the P2-002 candidates.",
    "Each T2 relevance set favours one retriever because of how its candidates were shown to raters.",
    "T3 is circular, since the gold and the filter share a parser, and the parser has a known defect on merged "
    "changes.",
    "The corpus holds only the current, amended text, so the system can flag a later amendment but cannot show the "
    "earlier wording.",
    "T4 is small, its questions were drafted by an LLM, and it has one rater; the RePASs repair is exploratory.",
    "All main LLM results come from a Flash-Lite-class model on free tiers, so they may not hold for stronger models.",
    "The tools provide decision support only and are not legal advice.",
])
R.h2("5.3 Applications")
R.bullets([
    "**Compliance teams of regulated entities** can generate an applicability register, change-impact log and "
    "compliance calendar for their entity class, with every row linked to the RBI paragraph.",
    "**Financial groups** with several regulated entities can use the cross-class differential to see where the rules "
    "for, say, a bank and its NBFC subsidiary diverge.",
    "**Internal and statutory auditors** get a reviewable audit pack with sign-off and a JSON audit trail.",
    "**Policy owners** can run a gap analysis that checks an internal policy against every applicable obligation.",
    "**Regulatory-technology vendors and researchers** can test RAG systems on Indian regulation, and metric designers "
    "can use the evidence on the limits of RePASs.",
    "**Teachers and trainers** can use the inspection website to show how LLMs fail on subtle regulatory differences.",
])
R.p("**Summary.** The project's strengths are a new evaluation target, a careful protocol and interpretable, "
    "privacy-preserving tools. Its main limitations are the size and single-expert nature of the gold set and the "
    "reliance on small hosted models. The tools are most useful to compliance teams, auditors, vendors and "
    "researchers.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Chapter 6
R.chapter_heading("Conclusion and Future Scope")
R.p("This chapter answers the three research questions and sets out the work that should follow.")
R.h2("6.1 Conclusion")
R.p("RBI-ObliBench tests whether systems can tell which RBI obligations bind an entity class and where parallel Master "
    "Directions differ. Its answers to the three research questions are as follows.")
R.p("**RQ1 (Applicability).** For retrieval, yes: a class filter raises counterpart nDCG@10 by +0.451. For judgement, "
    "the filter makes no significant difference on T1.")
R.p("**RQ2 (Differential obligation).** Not with current free-form LLM systems. Every system we evaluated, from "
    "embedding matching to long-context reading, misses most real differences, usually with the counterpart already "
    "in context. A structured comparator that decides by a fixed rule raises *different* F1 to 0.409 and recovers 26 "
    "of the 56 differences that every LLM missed, but it still misses 40 of the 78 gold *different* units.")
R.p("**RQ3 (Metric integrity).** Yes: RePASs rewards verbatim concatenation on RBI text as it does on ObliQA. A "
    "faithfulness metric therefore needs a relevance component that copied text cannot satisfy, and the repair we "
    "tried does not yet provide one without losing agreement with human ratings.")
R.p("Alongside these findings, the project produced a pointer-only data release, a tested code base, a public "
    "inspection website, a local compliance audit generator and a research paper submitted to AISIGH-SD 2026.")
R.h2("6.2 Future scope")
R.p("Table 6.1 ranks the work that would most improve the benchmark and the tools. The first two items address the "
    "reliability of the gold labels; the others extend the models, tasks and sources evaluated.")
@@B24@@

